; A macro's private bindings cannot swallow the caller's.
;
; `std-cheaper-than` binds `before` in its own body and then substitutes the
; caller's expressions inside that scope. Before macro hygiene this program
; asked the model `caller kept: 1` — the run's private count of model calls,
; leaked into a prompt in place of a binding the caller wrote and could see.
;
; The count is the structural half and the text is the semantic half: two
; firings for the arrow inside the space, one for the accepted branch, and the
; branch must carry the CALLER's value. A model cannot fake either.
; expect: calls 3
; expect: clean
; expect: contains caller kept: KEPT
; expect: answers
((# std/measure)
 (= before "Reply with exactly the word: KEPT"
    (std-cheaper-than 99
                      ("Reply with exactly the word: work")
                      ($ "caller kept: " before)
                      ("Reply with exactly the word: rejected"))))
